     hollow masks usually collapse into one-pixel-thick rings where D5 becomes\n-#      invisible, so excluding them keeps the fused effect observable.\n-#    - Placeholder colors are capped at 2-4 and each chosen color is used at\n-#      least twice inside the frame.  That keeps the scene readable and repeats\n-#      the same transformed hollow template multiple times.\n-#    - Every generated sprite must satisfy three checks simultaneously:\n-#      it has at least one enclosed hole, outlining removes at least one pixel,\n-#      and the outlined result is still non-empty.  This prevents silent\n-#      regression to plain D3 or plain D5 behavior.\n-#\n-# c) Old assumptions that must change:\n-#    - The original solver effectively assumed an outside sprite mask could be\n-#      written back directly.  That is no longer valid; the solver must rebuild\n-#      a mask, outline it, and only then stamp it.\n-#    - Hollow sprites introduce bg inside the bbox, but this does not break\n-#      placeholder detection because placeholders inside the frame remain solid\n-#      NxN blocks.\n-#    - Legend parsing still relies on compact two-color overlapping glyphs with\n-#      no bg in their union bbox.  Because sprites and legends are placed with a\n-#      full-bbox reserve mask, hollow sprite holes cannot leak into legend bboxes.\n-#    - The \"one size-matched sprite per placeholder color\" assumption still\n-#      holds because the generator keeps exactly one external template bbox for\n-#      each source color and keeps legends at fixed 3x4 size.\n-#\n-# d) Placement legality:\n-#    - Inside the frame, placeholders still live on an NxN grid with one-cell bg\n-#      gaps, so even outlined hollow sprites never overlap after stamping.\n-#    - Outside the frame, both legends and sprites reserve their full bboxes plus\n-#      padding, so no two patches overlap and no legend can sit inside a sprite\n-#      hole.\n-#    - The generator is output-first: it constructs the expected outlined-hollow\n-#      output frame, backfills the input placeholders and external cues, then\n-#      runs `main()` as an oracle.\n-#\n-# e) Visual observability:\n-#    - A human can see the hollow-source rule directly because every outside\n-#      sprite has an enclosed bg hole.  A human can see the outline-placement\n-#      rule because the frame output is thinner than the external template while\n-#      preserving the inner-hole boundary.\n-#    - No ordering depends on numeric color ids.  Colors only serve as visible\n-#      equality keys: placeholder color selects the source sprite, and legend\n-#      pairing selects the final recolor.\n-#\n-# f) Visual cleanliness:\n-#    - Sprites stay small and thick enough to show nested contours cleanly.\n-#      Reducing the number of placeholder colors avoids a noisy legend field.\n-#    - Targets are recolored uniformly per source color, and the frame border plus\n-#      bg gaps keep the transformed regions visually separated.\n-#\n-# g) Change inferability:\n-#    - The recoloring rule remains structurally explained by the same outside\n-#      legends as the original task, so the only new inference burden is local:\n-#      \"copy the hollow sprite, but keep only its contour pixels.\"\n-#    - Because each grid repeats source colors multiple times, the solver gets\n-#      repeated evidence that the same hollow template is reused consistently.\n-#\n-# Post-change solver assumptions reviewed against the generator:\n-#    - Every placeholder color has exactly one non-legend sprite bbox of the same\n-#      size: guaranteed.\n-#    - Every external sprite has >=1 enclosed hole: guaranteed by `_hole_count`.\n-#    - Every external sprite loses pixels under outlining: guaranteed by\n-#      `_outline_mask(mask).sum() < mask.sum()`.\n-#    - Outlined masks stay non-empty and fit exactly inside placeholder bboxes:\n-#      guaranteed.\n-#    - Legends use target colors outside the placeholder-color set, so legend\n-#      source/target inference remains unambiguous.\n+# Design analysis for the D3/1 + D5/3 combo variant\n+# --------------------------------------------------\n+# variant:\n+#   core_change:\n+#     原题把框外同色模板完整搬进框内，再按图例重着色。这里做两层叠加：\n+#     1) 框外模板本身已经是“空心精灵”，即其 bbox 内存在被同色像素完全包围的背景洞。\n+#     2) 搬运时不保留整块模板，而是对该空心模板取 4 邻域轮廓：任何上下左右四个方向\n+#        都还是前景的像素都会被删掉。\n+#     轮廓定义因此不是“只保留最外侧外形”，而是“保留所有与背景接触的前景像素”。\n+#     对空心模板来说，洞边界同样接触背景，所以内环和外环都会被保留，形成双轮廓。\n+#   concrete_behavior:\n+#     一个可视化小例子：若框外 5x5 精灵是\n+#       11111\n+#       11111\n+#       11011\n+#       11111\n+#       11111\n+#     原题会把这 24 个前景像素全贴进框内；只做 D3/1 时仍会得到同一空心块。\n+#     本融合版先承认中心 0 是封闭洞，再做 D5/3 轮廓化，于是外围方框保留、洞周围的\n+#     内边界也保留，而厚壁中间那些四邻都为 1 的像素会被删掉，出现“外环 + 内环”的\n+#     嵌套轮廓。这正是单独任何一项变体都不稳定产生的视觉结果。\n+#   complexity_guardrails:\n+#     - 若模板没有洞，融合会退化成普通轮廓放置。\n+#     - 若模板虽然有洞，但墙厚只有 1，则轮廓化后与原空心模板相同，D5/3 不可观察。\n+#     因此生成器强制每个模板同时满足：\n+#       * bbox 内至少有一个封闭背景洞；\n+#       * 轮廓化时至少会删掉一个像素；\n+#       * 轮廓结果仍同时包含外边界像素与洞边界像素。\n+#     这让 2-4 个样例就能稳定暴露“空心对象 + 嵌套轮廓”规律。\n+#   solver_assumption_audit:\n+#     - `_find_frame` 只依赖唯一矩形�[str, int]:\n-    edges = [\"top\", \"bottom\", \"left\", \"right\"]\n-    used_count = min(4, max(2, k))\n-    used_edges = random.sample(edges, used_count)\n-    counts = {edge: 0 for edge in edges}\n-    for edge in used_edges:\n-        counts[edge] = 1\n-    for _ in range(k - used_count):\n-        counts[random.choice(used_edges)] += 1\n-    return counts\n-\n-\n-def _legend_layout(\n-    src_cols: list[int], n: int, h: int, w: int\n-) -> dict[int, tuple[str, int]] | None:\n-    counts = _choose_edge_counts(len(src_cols))\n-    corner_clear = n + 1\n-    starts = {\n-        \"top\": _slot_starts(w, n, counts[\"top\"], corner_clear),\n-        \"bottom\": _slot_starts(w, n, counts[\"bottom\"], corner_clear),\n-        \"left\": _slot_starts(h, n, counts[\"left\"], corner_clear),\n-        \"right\": _slot_starts(h, n, counts[\"right\"], corner_clear),\n-    }\n-    if any(v is None for v in starts.values()):\n-        return None\n-\n-    order = src_cols[:]\n-    random.shuffle(order)\n-    layout: dict[int, tuple[str, int]] = {}\n-    idx = 0\n-    for edge in (\"top\", \"bottom\", \"left\", \"right\"):\n-        for start in starts[edge]:\n-            layout[order[idx]] = (edge, int(start))\n-            idx += 1\n-    assert idx == len(order)\n-    return layout\n-\n-\n-def _place_free_patch(\n-    grid: np.ndarray, reserve: np.ndarray, patch: np.ndarray, bg: int\n-) -> bool:\n+def _place_patch(grid: np.ndarray, reserve: np.ndarray, patch: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> bool:\n     blocker = np.full(patch.shape, FRAME_COL, dtype=int)\n     try:\n-        r, c = random_free_location(reserve, blocker, transparent=bg, padding=PATCH_PADDING, border=1)\n+        r, c = random_free_location(reserve, blocker, transparent=bg, padding=PATCH_PADDING)\n     except ValueError:\n         return False\n     paste(reserve, blocker, r, c)\n     paste(grid, patch, r, c, transparent=bg)\n     return True\n-\n \n-def _collect_placeholders(\n-    crop: np.ndarray, bg: int, frame_col: int\n-) -> tuple[list[tuple[int, tuple[int, int, int, int]]], dict[int, tuple[int, int]], set[int]]:\n-    h, w = crop.shape\n-    inner_mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=bool)\n-    inner_mask[1:-1, 1:-1] = True\n-    placeholder_colors = set(map(int, np.unique(crop[inner_mask]))) - {bg, frame_col}\n-    assert placeholder_colors, \"no placeholder colors found inside frame\"\n \n-    placeholders: list[tuple[int, tuple[int, int, int, int]]] = []\n-    for col in sorted(placeholder_colors):\n-        for pts in components_mask((crop == col) & inner_mask, connectivity=4):\n-            r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n-            placeholders.append((col, (r0, c0, r1, c1)))\n-\n-    phw: dict[int, tuple[int, int]] = {}\n-    for col in placeholder_colors:\n-        dims = [(b[2] - b[0] + 1, b[3] - b[1] + 1) for c, b in placeholders if c == col]\n-        assert